values
  ('telling-people-about-a-diagnosis', 'Telling People About a Diagnosis', 'The second job nobody prepares you for: deciding who gets told and how much. Why nobody is owed this, how to sort people into circles, and what to do with the advice that follows.', 'The diagnosis has arrived. Now there is a second, separate job nobody prepares you for: deciding who gets told, how much, and when.

And people will have opinions about that too, which is part of what makes it hard. The information is yours. How it travels is a set of choices you are allowed to make deliberately.

Nobody is owed this

Start here, because it is the thing most people need permission for.

Your health information belongs to you. Not to your parents, not to your team, not to the group chat. There is no obligation to tell anybody, and no deadline for it.

The only exceptions are practical rather than moral: where somebody needs to know to keep you safe, where a condition is genuinely passed down and a relative could act on knowing, or where your employer needs a fact to arrange something you want.

Everything else is your call, and it can be made slowly.

Sort people into circles

The useful move is to stop treating this as one decision and make it several.

The inner circle gets the whole thing: one or two people, the full detail, the frightening parts.

The next circle gets the headline and the practical: I have a condition, this is what it means for the next few months, here is what would help.

The wide circle gets almost nothing, or a vaguer version: I am dealing with a health thing and I am well looked after.

Write the names down if your head is full. People find that enormously settling, because the question stops being whether to tell and becomes which circle somebody is in.

Decide your sentences in advance

You will be asked at bad moments, by people you had not planned for, and whatever you say the first time tends to become your script.

So write two versions. The short one: I am dealing with a health thing, thanks for asking, I would rather not go into it. The longer one for the people who get detail.

Then practice both out loud once. It feels silly and it means you will not be improvising at a party.

And decide what you will say to the question people cannot resist: what is the prognosis. A good answer is: I am not discussing numbers, and I will tell you if there is news.

Expect the reactions to be about them

This catches people out. You deliver news and somebody panics, cries, offers a diet, tells you about their aunt, or goes strangely quiet.

Almost none of that is a comment on you. It is what happens when people are frightened and have no script, and the worst reactions often come from the people who care most.

You do not have to manage it. You are allowed to say I cannot talk about this today, and leave the room.

The one reaction worth planning for is the advice. Somebody will send you something. A flat sentence helps: I am following my doctors on treatment. That is said once, and repeated unchanged.

Handle work as a separate question

Work needs its own thinking, because the rules there are different.

You generally do not have to say what you have. You do have to say what you need, if you want adjustments: time off, a changed schedule, a lighter load, somewhere to sit.

So separate the two. The diagnosis is private. The accommodation is a request, and it can be made without naming anything: for medical reasons I need this for the next few months.

Find out what your workplace actually offers before the conversation, and put the request in writing afterward. Occupational health, HR or an employee assistance line can tell you what exists, and they are usually more helpful than people expect.

Control the spread

Information moves, and it moves faster than anybody intends.

So say what you want out loud when you tell somebody. I am telling you and I would rather you did not pass it on. People generally respect that when it is said plainly, and generally assume the opposite when it is not.

Decide about the internet separately. A post ends the exhausting business of telling people one at a time, and it cannot be taken back. Plenty of people are glad they did it. Plenty wish they had waited a month.

If you have children, decide together with the other adults what they are told, and keep it simple and true rather than cheerful.

Let it change over time

The first version of your telling is not binding. People widen the circle as they get used to it, and sometimes narrow it again when they find the conversations tiring.

Both are allowed. You can tell somebody more later, and you can stop giving updates to anybody who has turned your health into their hobby.

When to get help

Please ask your medical team the questions you have not asked yet, however small, and ask whether a nurse specialist or a patient advocate is available to you, since they often have more time than the doctor and know what help exists locally. Talk to a therapist if the weeks since the diagnosis have been sleepless or frightening, which is extremely common and is treated well alongside your other care, and ask your team whether there is a counselor attached to the service. A support group with the same condition is worth a try, and so is one for your family, who are carrying something too and often have nowhere to put it.', array['Life Transitions', 'Boundaries']::text[], 0, '2026-11-06T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-sleep-changes-with-age', 'When Your Sleep Changes With Age', 'Awake at half past four and the night feels broken. What actually changes with age, why counting the hours makes it worse, and which things are not just getting older.', 'You used to sleep through. Now you are awake at half past four, properly awake, and the night feels broken. Or you drop off at nine in front of the television and then cannot settle at eleven.

Nothing has gone wrong. Sleep changes shape as people get older, and knowing what is normal here saves a great deal of unnecessary worry.

What actually changes

Several things, all at once, which is why it feels like more than it is.

Sleep gets lighter. The deep stretch at the start of the night shortens, so noises, a full bladder or a warm room wake you where once they would not have.

The timing shifts earlier. Many people get sleepy earlier in the evening and wake earlier in the morning, and that shift is a real change in the internal clock rather than a habit.

Waking in the night becomes normal. Brief wakings happen at every age and you start noticing them, and remembering them, more.

And the total need barely changes. Most older adults still need roughly what they always did, but it arrives in a more broken form and often with a nap in it.

Stop counting the hours

A great deal of the distress here is arithmetic rather than tiredness.

People wake at four, calculate that they have had five hours, conclude the day is ruined, and then lie there in a state that guarantees no more sleep.

So take the clock out of the bedroom, or turn it away. If you wake, the only question worth asking is whether you feel sleepy, not what time it is.

And judge your sleep by the day rather than the night. If you get through the afternoon without a struggle, your sleep is doing its job whatever shape it came in.

Work with the earlier clock rather than against it

If your body now wants to sleep at nine and wake at four, you can shift that a little, and fighting it entirely tends to fail.

The lever is light. Get bright light in the late afternoon and early evening, outdoors if possible, which nudges the clock later. Keep the evening lit rather than dim until bedtime.

Avoid the evening doze in the chair, which is what steals the sleepiness you need at eleven. If you cannot stay awake in front of the television, go to bed, or do something upright instead.

In the morning, if you are waking very early, go easy on bright light in the first hour, which is the opposite of the usual advice and the right version for this problem.

Make the night less interruptible

Since sleep is lighter now, the small things matter more than they used to.

Cool room, dark room, earplugs or steady sound. Less fluid in the last two hours, and a trip to the bathroom right before bed. A clear path to the bathroom with a dim light, because a bright one wakes you fully.

Look at what you take. Some blood pressure drugs, diuretics, steroids and antidepressants disturb sleep or send you to the bathroom at night, and the timing of a dose can often be changed. That is a conversation with your doctor rather than something to put up with.

Alcohol gets worse with age as a sleep aid, not better. It still puts you under and it breaks the second half of the night more than it used to.

Use the wakeful hour rather than lying in it

If you are awake and not sleepy, get up. Lying there awake teaches your body that bed is a place for being awake, which is the main thing that turns a change into insomnia.

So go and sit somewhere else, in low light, with something dull. Go back when you feel heavy. People who do this consistently find the wakings get shorter.

And if you are reliably up at five, consider making it a good hour rather than a bad one. Plenty of people find it the best, quietest part of their day once they stop resenting it.

Know what is not just age

Age explains lighter and earlier. It does not explain everything, and these are worth a doctor rather than acceptance.

Loud snoring, gasping, or being told you stop breathing. Falling asleep in the daytime without meaning to. Legs that crawl or ache and must be moved. Acting out dreams, shouting or thrashing. Pain that wakes you. Needing the bathroom many times a night.

And low mood, which both disturbs sleep and gets blamed on it. Early-morning waking with a flat, heavy feeling is a different thing from a shifted clock.

When to get help

Please see your doctor if you snore heavily or wake gasping, if you fall asleep in the daytime without meaning to, if your legs will not let you settle, or if you act out your dreams, because each of those is a specific and treatable condition rather than part of getting older. Ask about your medications and their timing, which is one of the most common fixable causes. If you have lain awake for months, ask specifically about cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia, which works better than sleeping pills and lasts longer, and is suitable at any age. And tell your doctor if your mood has been low alongside it, since the two are tangled and treating one helps the other.', array['Sleep', 'Body']::text[], 0, '2026-11-06T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('talking-to-your-parents-about-their-money', 'Talking to Your Parents About Their Money', 'Unopened letters and no idea what their finances look like. How to open it without sounding like you are taking over, what to ask instead of balances, and when it has become urgent.', 'You have noticed the unopened letters. Or the renewal that got paid twice. Or they mentioned, lightly, that somebody called about an investment. And you have no idea what their finances actually look like, because you have never asked and they have never said.

It is one of the hardest conversations in a family, and the reason it gets postponed is simple: it touches money, independence, and the fact that your parents are getting older, all at once.

Know why you are asking

Being clear with yourself first makes the conversation much easier, because the suspicion people have is that you are after the money or taking over.

The honest reasons are usually: so nobody has to guess in a crisis, so bills do not go unpaid, so somebody can help if they are scammed, and so their wishes get followed rather than improvised.

Say that out loud when you start. I am not trying to take anything over. I want to know enough that if something happened, I could help the way you would want.

Start with the smallest possible ask

The whole-picture conversation almost always fails on the first attempt. Something narrower works.

So ask for one thing. Where do you keep the important papers. Who is your doctor. Is there somebody who does your taxes. If you were in the hospital for a week, who would need to be called.

Then stop. Having asked one thing successfully is the whole goal of the first conversation, and it makes the second one far easier.

Pick a moment that is not a crisis and not a celebration. A quiet weekday, a car journey, a walk. Not a birthday, and not the day after a fall.

Use somebody else''s situation as the door

The least threatening opening is almost never about them.

I have been sorting out my own will and it made me realize I have no idea about yours. My friend''s father went into the hospital and nobody could find anything. I am doing this for myself and thought I would ask what you did.

That makes the subject a shared practical matter rather than an assessment of their competence, which is what they are braced for.

Ask about the system, not the balances

You almost never need to know how much they have. You need to know how it works.

Where the papers are. Which bank. Whether bills are on automatic payment. Whether anyone else can sign anything. Whether there is a will, where it is, and who the attorney or executor is. Who advises them, if anybody.

Framed that way it is a logistics conversation, and most people answer logistics questions willingly where they would refuse a question about amounts.

Respect the no, and leave the door open

If the answer is no, take it. Pressing turns a postponement into a refusal, and it can poison the subject for years.

So say the thing that keeps it alive. That is fine. Can I ask you again in a few months. In the meantime, is there one thing you would want me to know.

And know the difference between a parent who does not want to discuss it and a parent who cannot manage it. The first is their right. The second needs a different approach, and probably a doctor.

Watch for the signs that this has become urgent

Most of this can go at their pace. Some things cannot.

Unopened mail piling up. Services cut off for non-payment. Large or odd withdrawals. A new friend, tradesman or caller who has become very involved. Confusion about money they used to handle easily. Shame or secrecy that is new.

Financial exploitation of older people is common and is more often somebody known to them than a stranger. Be careful about saying so out loud too fast, since the usual response is defensiveness, and get advice before you confront anyone.

Mind the siblings

A great deal of the damage in this area is done between brothers and sisters rather than with the parent.

So tell them before you talk to your parent, not after. Agree who asks. Write down what you learn and share it. Avoid becoming the one who knows everything, which breeds suspicion however well you behave.

And keep your parent''s wishes at the center rather than the family''s preferences. The question is what they want, not what everybody would find convenient.

When to get help

Please encourage them toward their own professional rather than relying on family: an attorney for a will and a power of attorney, and an accountant or a financial adviser they choose themselves, which protects both of them and you. If you suspect a scam or exploitation, contact adult protective services or your bank''s fraud team for advice before confronting anybody, since both deal with this constantly. See your parent''s doctor if the money trouble comes with confusion, forgetting or a change in judgment, because that is a medical question first. And talk to a therapist yourself if this has become a source of dread or family conflict, since the role reversal underneath it is a real grief and worth somewhere to put.', array['Money', 'Caregiving']::text[], 0, '2026-11-06T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-are-angry-at-someone-who-died', 'When You Are Angry at Someone Who Died', 'Furious with them and nowhere to put it, while everyone around you is being gentle. Why this is ordinary, how to find what it is actually about, and why forgiveness is not required.', 'They died, and you are furious with them. For the thing they said and never took back. For the money, or the will, or the way they treated your mother. For smoking for forty years. For leaving in a way that has made your life much harder.

And there is nowhere to put it, because you cannot have the argument, and because everybody around you is being gentle about a person you are not feeling gentle about.

This is extremely common and rarely admitted

Anger is a standard part of grief and it is the part people keep to themselves, because saying it out loud feels like a betrayal of the dead and an embarrassment in front of the living.

So the first thing worth knowing is that this is ordinary. Being angry at somebody who died does not mean you did not love them, and it does not mean you are grieving wrongly.

It often comes with guilt attached, and the guilt is what makes it so heavy. You are angry, then ashamed of being angry, then angry at being made to feel ashamed, and the whole thing goes round privately for months.

Find out what it is actually about

Anger in grief is usually specific, and naming the specific thing brings it down to a size you can hold.

So write it plainly. I am angry that he never apologized. I am angry that she chose not to see the doctor. I am angry that he left the finances in this state. I am angry that she was the only person who knew me and now nobody does.

Sometimes, underneath, the anger is not at them at all. It is at the hospital, at a sibling, at the unfairness of it, or at your own choices. Sorting that out does not remove the anger and it does put it in the right place.

And sometimes the anger is the only safe feeling available. If what is underneath is unbearable grief, anger can be what your mind reaches for instead, because it has energy in it and sorrow does not.

Say it to somebody who will not flinch

The reason this festers is almost always that it has never been spoken.

So pick one person who can hear it without defending them. A friend outside the family, a counselor, a support group, a helpline. Not, usually, somebody who loved them equally, who will be hurt by it.

If there is nobody, write it. A letter to them, unsent, said exactly as you mean it, with no editing for kindness. People regularly report that doing this once changes something that years of carrying it did not.

Hold both without resolving them

The push you will feel, from yourself and from others, is to settle it: to decide they were good and let the anger go, or to decide they were bad and let the grief go.

Neither works, because both are usually true at once.

So practice the sentence with the and in it. He was cruel to me and I miss him. She did real damage and she was the only one who came when I was ill. That is not a contradiction you have failed to resolve. It is an accurate description of a person.

Do not wait for forgiveness

Forgiveness gets prescribed a great deal here, often by people who are uncomfortable with your anger.

You may get there and you may not, and you do not need to in order to live well. What matters is that the anger stops running your weeks, and that can happen with or without forgiving anything.

So aim lower and more usefully: less time, less grip, fewer ambushes. Not a changed verdict.

Give it somewhere physical to go

Anger sits in the body, and grief anger particularly so, which is why so many people in this position describe being wound up with nowhere to aim it.

So walk fast, swim, dig, lift something heavy, clean something properly. Not as a metaphor. As a way of spending what is in your system.

And watch the drinking, which is the most common way people in this spot take the edge off, and which reliably makes the anger worse and the sleep shorter.

Expect it at the dates

Anniversaries, birthdays and the ordinary ambushes bring it back, often sharper than the sadness.

That is not going backward. Grief is not linear, and anger tends to arrive in waves separated by stretches where you had thought it was finished.

When to get help

Please talk to a grief counselor or a therapist if the anger is still running your weeks many months on, if it is leaking onto people who did not deserve it, or if what you are grieving is somebody who hurt you, which is a particular and complicated kind of loss that is much easier with help than alone. Ask your doctor if you are not sleeping, if you are drinking more, or if your mood has been low for weeks. And if you are having thoughts of not wanting to be here, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Anger', 'Grief']::text[], 0, '2026-11-06T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-look-back-at-your-own-work-and-cringe', 'When You Look Back at Your Own Work and Cringe', 'You open the old file and your whole body contracts. Why the cringe is evidence of growth, what to keep and what to take down, and the version of it that stops you making things.', 'You find the file. The thing you wrote three years ago, the photographs from the project you were proud of, the recording, the code, the painting. You open it expecting to feel something warm and instead your whole body contracts.

How did I think that was good. Who let me send that out.

The cringe is a sign of distance, not of failure

Here is the thing worth understanding first, because it reframes the whole experience.

You can only see what is wrong with that work because you know more now. The gap you are feeling is the distance you have traveled, measured in the only way it can be measured.

Which means the cringe is evidence of growth. Unpleasant evidence, arriving in the body, but evidence.

And the opposite would be worse. If your work from three years ago still looked perfect to you, that would mean three years of nothing.

Separate the work from the person

The cringe usually says two things at once, and only one of them is true.

The true part: that work had real weaknesses, and I can see them now.

The part that sneaks in: and therefore I am an embarrassment, and everyone who saw it thought so.

The second does not follow. Work has levels. People do not. Practice saying the first without the second, which is most of the skill here.

Remember what you could actually see at the time

You are judging past work with present eyes, which is an unfair contest nobody can win.

So ask the fairer question: was that good for what I knew and had then. Usually it was. Often it was the best you could do that year, with that much skill, in that much time, under whatever was going on in your life.

That is not a consolation prize. It is the accurate standard, and it is the one you would apply to anybody else.

Know that almost nobody noticed

The particular agony of this is imagining everybody who saw it and judged it.

They did not, mostly. They read it, used it, enjoyed it or forgot it, and moved on within a day. The flaws that are enormous to you are invisible to people who were not inside the making of it.

And the people who did notice are generally kinder than you are. They have their own old work.

Decide what to do with it, deliberately

The cringe pushes toward deleting everything, and that is usually the wrong move in the morning.

So sort it. Things that are actively wrong or harmful, and still live where people find them: fix or take down. Things that are merely worse than you would do now: leave them. Things that are private: keep them, because they are a record.

Keeping some of it is worth more than you think. A folder of old work is the clearest proof you have that you improve, and it is the thing you will want on the day you are convinced you have not.

Use it rather than just enduring it

There is real information in the cringe, if you look at it once rather than flinching away.

So name the specific thing. Too long. Overexplained. Trying to sound clever. Afraid to make a claim. Copying somebody''s style. Whatever it is, it is probably a habit you still have a trace of, and seeing it clearly in old work is the easiest place to catch it.

Then stop. One pass, one lesson, close the file. Rereading it eleven times is not analysis.

Watch for the version that stops you making things

Mild cringe is healthy. There is a version that does damage, and it is worth catching.

If looking back makes you unable to publish, send or show anything new, the standard has stopped being useful and started being a brake. If you have deleted work you now wish you had, that is a sign too. If the feeling is less I have improved and more I am a fraud who got away with it, that is a different problem from craft.

And notice the long view. People who keep making things describe cringing at their work for decades, in a cycle, while the work gets steadily better. The cringe is not the thing that goes away. The paralysis is.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if looking back at your own work reliably tips into feeling worthless rather than simply wincing, or if the fear of making something you will later cringe at is stopping you making anything at all, because that is perfectionism rather than standards and it responds well to treatment. It is also worth a conversation if the shame attached to old work or old mistakes goes far wider than the work, which usually means it connects to something older. And see your doctor if your mood has been low for weeks, since a low mood makes every backward look harsher than it needs to be.', array['Perfectionism', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-11-06T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
